+# Vault Auto-unseal Failover Test (runbook)
+
+Objective:
+- Validate Vault HA + PKCS#11 auto-unseal works in failover scenarios (node replacement, pod eviction).
+
+Prerequisites:
+- CloudHSM cluster provisioned and accessible to Vault hosts.
+- Vault Helm deployed with PKCS#11 values (vault/helm/values-hsm-committed.yaml).
+- Vault service account and policies created for operator access.
+- kubectl access to the cluster.
+
+Steps:
+1. Verify current cluster unseal status:
+   kubectl -n vault exec -it vault-0 -- vault status
+   Expect: "Sealed: false" and HA mode "active" or "standby".
+
+2. Identify a Vault pod to evict (not the active leader, if possible):
+   kubectl -n vault get pods -l app=vault
+
+3. Evict the pod:
+   kubectl -n vault drain <vault-node> or kubectl -n vault delete pod vault-1
+
+4. Monitor replacement pod logs:
+   kubectl -n vault logs -f <new-pod> -c vault
+   Look for PKCS#11 auto-unseal operations and success messages.
+
+5. Validate new pod is unsealed:
+   kubectl -n vault exec -it <new-pod> -- vault status
+   Expect: "Sealed: false"
+
+6. Repeat for multiple pods and simulate leader failure:
+   a) Scale down active pod to force leader election.
+   b) Ensure new leader unseals with HSM.
+
+7. Run validation script (vault/scripts/validate_unseal.py) to assert seal/unseal transitions and audit presence.
+
+Recovery / rollback:
+- If auto-unseal fails, collect vault logs and HSM vendor logs, re-run manual unseal with init keys (follow emergency procedures).
+
+Post-test:
+- Ensure audit logs have recorded key usage; forward logs to SIEM for archiving.
+
