+# Rekor Restore Drill & TLS Cert Provisioning Runbook
+
+Objectives:
+- Verify backup/restore of Rekor DB (production RDS) and validate Rekor server functionality after restore.
+- Provision TLS certificates for Rekor API and rotate certificates safely.
+
+Prerequisites:
+- S3 backup bucket with recent Rekor DB dump (pg_dump -Fc).
+- Access to a sandbox Postgres instance or staging RDS to restore into.
+- kubectl access to rekor namespace in cluster.
+- openssl installed for cert generation (or use your PKI).
+
+Restore drill steps:
+1. Identify backup file in S3 (rekor backups). Download locally:
+   aws s3 cp s3://{bucket}/{backup-file} /tmp/rekor.dump
+
+2. Restore to staging Postgres:
+   PGPASSWORD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD} pg_restore -h ${STAGING_PG_HOST} -U ${POSTGRES_USER} -d rekor /tmp/rekor.dump
+
+3. Update rekor Deployment in staging to point to staging DB and restart.
+
+4. Validate Rekor API:
+   curl -k "https://rekor.staging.internal/api/v1/log/log_info"
+   curl -k "https://rekor.staging.internal/api/v1/log/entries?logIndex=<some-index>"
+
+5. If validation passes, document times and any data differences; notify stakeholders.
+
+TLS certificate provisioning:
+1. For testing: generate self-signed cert (rekor/scripts/provision_tls_certs.sh).
+2. For production: request cert from enterprise CA and store in Kubernetes TLS Secret:
+   kubectl -n rekor create secret tls rekor-tls --cert=rekor.crt --key=rekor.key
+3. Restart rekor deployment to pick up TLS secret (if ingress uses secret).
+
+Rollback & cleanup:
+- If restore causes unexpected issues, revert Deployment to previous image/environment and restore DB snapshot.
+
